From 8a7dd8e2c055daaea571c0852a32b75ebceaf059 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Shine <mark@ankra.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:28:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(loadbalancer): stop replacing healthy load balancers, and
 reapply ones that stop forwarding

A config annotation that sets one backend property, such as
{"outbound_proxy_protocol": "v2"}, replaced the whole default properties
object, so every other property was sent as its zero value. The API filled
in its own defaults, the live load balancer never compared equal to the
desired one, and every leader change logged "backends fields differ" and
replaced the whole load balancer configuration. On 2026-11-15 one of those
replaces, during a control-plane stall, left a production load balancer
accepting connections on 80/443 without forwarding a packet to any member
for 43 minutes. Replacing it again with the same configuration fixed it.

Three changes:

- Annotation properties on backends and frontends are laid over the
  defaults field by field, so a partial properties object keeps every
  default it does not name, and an unchanged service compares equal.
  Shared *bool defaults are copied first so one field cannot set another.

- An update that would leave a backend with no members, while the live
  load balancer has some, is refused with an error instead of pushed. The
  controller only builds an empty backend when it has lost sight of the
  nodes, and pushing one takes a working load balancer down silently.

- The elected leader runs a forwarding monitor. Every two minutes it reads
  GET /load-balancer/{uuid}/metrics for each LoadBalancer Service the
  controller has bound. A running load balancer with a backend that has
  enabled members but none reported up on two checks in a row has its
  current configuration reapplied, at most once per ten minutes, with a
  LoadBalancerNotForwarding warning event on the Service. The service
  controller never notices this state on its own: it reconciles only on a
  Service change, a node set change or a restart, and it compares
  configurations, not traffic.

Also fixes loadBalancerToCreateRequest writing backend TLS configs at the
backend index instead of the config index.
